ctlsock: never hand a narrow-accumulator device a delta it reads backwards

Applies AFTER mame-ctlsock-ptr-tags.patch (it needs m_ptr_mod).

A device whose mouse ioport is narrower than the module's default 16-bit
accumulator differences successive field reads inside a window of +-mod/2.
The Apple II Mouse Card is the worst case: src/devices/bus/a2bus/mouse.cpp,
a2bus_mouse_device::update_axis(), reads the 8-bit IPT_MOUSE_X field and folds

	int diff = new_m - m_last[AXIS];
	if (diff > 0x80) diff -= 0x100; else if (diff < -0x80) diff += 0x100;

so ONE field write of +200 with MAME_CTL_PTR_MOD=256 is read as -56 and the
pointer runs the WRONG WAY. `MOVE` was the only verb that could produce such a
write: it called move_rel() directly, while MOVEP and MOVEA travel already went
through the paced queue (<= MAME_CTL_MOVE_STEP counts per 40 ms window).

Measured on apple2e (Dazzle Draw canvas, golden.sta, 1.547 px/count on X), one
MOVE per trial from the left clamp, arrow x measured from the framebuffer:

	MOVE  10 -> 36 px   MOVE 100 -> 171 px   MOVE 130 -> no movement
	MOVE  20 -> 50 px   MOVE 120 -> 207 px   MOVE 160 -> no movement
	MOVE  40 -> 79 px   MOVE 127 -> 217 px   MOVE 200 -> no movement
	MOVE  60 -> 109 px                       MOVE 255 -> no movement

Perfectly linear to 127, dead from 129 (128 is diff == 0x80, still accepted).
The same 200 and 400 counts issued as 2x100 / 4x100 -- what this patch makes
the module do internally -- moved 204 and 409 counts' worth, back-to-back with
no delay, because the card samples port B at least once per emulated frame.

Two hunks: cap the pacing budget at the differencing window whenever the
accumulator is narrow, and route an oversized MOVE onto the paced queue.

--- a/src/osd/modules/ctlsock/ctlsock.cpp
+++ b/src/osd/modules/ctlsock/ctlsock.cpp
@@ -409,6 +409,16 @@
 		m_ptr_mod = env_long("MAME_CTL_PTR_MOD", 65536);
 		if (m_ptr_mod < 256 || m_ptr_mod > 65536)
 			m_ptr_mod = 65536;
+		// A narrow accumulator has a DIFFERENCING WINDOW: the device reads
+		// the field and folds new-last into +-mod/2 (a2bus mouse.cpp
+		// update_axis(): `if (diff > 0x80) diff -= 0x100`). One field write
+		// larger than mod/2 is therefore read as motion the OTHER WAY.
+		// Measured on apple2e/Dazzle Draw (PTR_MOD=256, 1.55 px/count):
+		// MOVE 127 -> +197 px, MOVE 130/160/200/255 -> 0 px (the arrow ran
+		// LEFT into the clamp). Cap the pacing budget at the window so the
+		// queue never hands the device an ambiguous delta.
+		if (m_ptr_mod < 65536)
+			m_move_step = std::max(1L, std::min(m_move_step, (m_ptr_mod / 2) - 1));
 		split3(env_str("MAME_CTL_BTN_NAMES"), m_btn_name[0], m_btn_name[1], m_btn_name[2]);
 		if (m_btn_name[0].empty() && m_btn_name[1].empty() && m_btn_name[2].empty())
 		{
@@ -2610,7 +2620,16 @@
 			++m_c_move;
 			++m_p_move;
 			input_seen();
-			move_rel(dx, dy);
+			// MOVE is the IMMEDIATE verb -- but "immediate" cannot mean one
+			// field write past the device's differencing window: an oversized
+			// single write is read BACKWARDS (see MAME_CTL_PTR_MOD above), so
+			// it goes on the paced queue instead, which emits <= m_move_step
+			// per window and is what MOVEP and MOVEA travel already use. The
+			// ack stays immediate -- MOVE has never acked on drain.
+			if ((std::abs(dx) > m_move_step) || (std::abs(dy) > m_move_step))
+				move_paced(dx, dy);
+			else
+				move_rel(dx, dy);
 			hist_apply(ack);
 			reply_ok(ack);
 		}
